<h2>What color temperature means</h2>
<p class="lead">Color temperature, measured in Kelvin (K), describes how warm or cool white light looks. Lower numbers glow amber like candlelight; higher numbers look blue-white like daylight.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Kelvin</th><th>Look</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>2200K</td><td>Amber, candle-like</td><td>String lights, historic or rustic settings, red brick</td></tr>
<tr><td>2700K</td><td>Warm white</td><td>Most homes: brick, warm stone, wood, entries</td></tr>
<tr><td>3000K</td><td>Soft white</td><td>Limestone, light stucco, many trees and full-property designs</td></tr>
<tr><td>4000K</td><td>Cool white</td><td>Moonlighting, blue-green or silver foliage, modern architecture</td></tr>
<tr><td>5000K</td><td>Daylight</td><td>Rarely flattering on homes; special effects only</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Consistency matters more than the number</h2>
<p>Mixing 2700K and 3000K by accident across a facade is more noticeable than either choice on its own. Pick a primary temperature, keep it consistent within each view, and use deliberate exceptions (like cooler moonlighting in a tree canopy). Color rendering (CRI) matters too: higher-CRI fixtures make plants look green and brick look rich rather than muddy.</p>
<p>For the full design discussion, read <a href="/warm-white-vs-cool-white-landscape-lighting/">Warm White vs. Cool White</a>, then try combinations in the <a href="/tools/lighting-visualizer/">lighting visualizer</a>. Spring Landscape Lighting tests color on site at night before finalizing <a href="/services/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting</a>.</p>
